  ### 1. The Backend & Portals Backbone: Local Docker Compose

  Use your existing docker-compose.yml:

  • Services running:
      • PostgreSQL (15-Alpine): Pre-loaded with your schema.sql and seed.sql.
      • FastAPI Backend: Exposing API endpoints at http://localhost:8000.
      • Nginx Reverse Proxy: Exposing port 80 to route both APIs and web portals.
  • Why it’s the best:
      • Uses less than 350 MB RAM for the entire backend stack.
      • Replicates exact production network topologies (Nginx proxy rules, headers,
      CORS).
      • Instant Reset: If test data gets messy, one command (docker compose down -v &&
      docker compose up -d) resets everything to clean seed state in 5 seconds.

  ──────
  ### 2. Testing Portals: Multi-Role Browser Session

  Open your standard browser (Chrome or Firefox) in split-screen:

  • Window 1 (Admin Portal): http://localhost/admin/ — Monitor platform metrics, audit
  logs, and approval queues.
  • Window 2 (Vendor Portal): http://localhost/vendor/ — Manage inventory, add
  hotels/guides/tours, and confirm bookings.
  • Window 3 (Consumer Web): http://localhost/consumer/ — Test public customer booking
  flows.
  ──────
  ### 3. Simulating the Mobile App: Flutter Web or USB Phone

  For the mobile application (mobile_app), avoid Android Studio and choose one of two
  paths:

  #### Option A: Flutter Web with Chrome DevTools Device Mode (Fastest)

  • How it works: Run flutter run -d chrome.
  • In Chrome, press F12 followed by Ctrl + Shift + M to switch into mobile viewport
  mode (e.g., Pixel 7 / iPhone 14 layout).
  • Why it's great:
      • Your ApiService is already coded to route to http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/v1 when
      running on web.
      • Uses only ~250 MB of RAM.
      • You can place all three views (Admin, Vendor, Mobile) on one screen
      simultaneously.


  #### Option B: Physical Phone + scrcpy (Closest to Real Production)

  • How it works: Plug in your Android phone via USB, run scrcpy on Linux to view the
  phone screen on your monitor, and run flutter run.
  • Forward the API port so the phone can reach your local Docker container:
    adb reverse tcp:8000 tcp:8000

  • Why it's great: Zero emulation overhead on your computer, but tests actual touch
  feel, mobile networking, and native performance.
  ──────
  ### Why this is the Best Workflow for End-to-End Simulation:

  1. Total System Footprint: ~1.0 to 1.2 GB RAM across the entire stack (compared to 8+
  GB with Android Studio + Emulator).
  2. True Closed-Loop Verification:
      • Step 1: Create/modify a travel package in the Vendor Portal.
      • Step 2: Inspect approval/status in the Admin Portal.
      • Step 3: Open the Mobile App, find the package, and book it.
      • Step 4: Watch the booking update live across both the Vendor dashboard and
      Admin metrics.